AB  - Diffusion language models (DLMs) can decode several tokens in one step.However, IG-CD and LAVE verify candidate tokens sequentially within each denoising step, introducing repeated sampling and grammar checks.A parser identifies the first invalid token while preserving the accepted prefix.We propose Dgrammar, a training-free decoder that repairs this frontier token using the current denoising scores.The method combines local repair with a grammargated batch schedule, asynchronous mask construction, and bounded grammar-guided completion.On JSONSchemaBench medium with LLaDA-8B, Dgrammar improves schema validity by 8.6 percentage points over LAVE, reduces mean latency by 5.8×, and eliminates timeouts.Validity and latency gains also hold on the other JSB splits and on Dream-7B.Experiments with a SMILES grammar demonstrate that the decoder also supports constraints beyond JSON-Schema.
