AB  - Tool-using LLM agents interact with external systems by generating structured API calls, yet these calls are often brittle: small local errors (malformed serialization, missing fields, type mismatches, or invalid values) can block execution even when the intent is correct. We propose a diffusion-based local repair framework that improves tool-call reliability without full regeneration. A frozen backbone agent produces an initial call; deterministic schema validation and executability checks then identify invalid fields and construct an edit mask that freezes the tool name, keys, and validated arguments while exposing only erroneous value spans. A diffusion language model performs span-restricted denoising to repair the masked regions with minimal unintended edits. We evaluate on a single-turn tool-invocation benchmark simulated from ATIS, SNIPS, and SLURP (intent-to-API and slot-to-argument mapping) under four perturbations: JSON corruption, missing fields, type mismatches, and mixed errors. Results show consistent gains in argument accuracy and call correctness over autoregressive regeneration baselines, with fewer modifications.
