AB  - The rapid advancement of large language models has led to the proliferation of autonomous agents capable of executing complex tasks. However, providing these agents with persistent, long-term memory remains a significant computational and architectural challenge. Traditional approaches rely on expanding the context window of the primary large language model or utilizing naive vector database retrieval systems. Both methods suffer from inefficiencies, either incurring prohibitive computational costs or failing to contextualize retrieved information accurately. This paper proposes a novel architectural paradigm wherein a small language model is deployed specifically as a dedicated memory controller. Operating asynchronously from the primary reasoning agent, the small language model manages the encoding, consolidation, summarization, and targeted retrieval of episodic and semantic memories. By offloading memory management to a lightweight, highly optimized auxiliary model, the proposed system drastically reduces the inference overhead on the primary agent while simultaneously enhancing the coherence and relevance of the retrieved context. Comprehensive evaluations demonstrate that this dual-model architecture reduces operational latency, minimizes token consumption, and significantly improves the retention of long-term task coherence. The findings indicate a crucial paradigm shift in agent design, moving from monolithic single-model systems toward specialized, multi-model cognitive architectures tailored for deployment in resource-constrained environments
