AB  - Abstract An assistant used every day needs to remember what the user told it, even when those facts change over time, and it needs to say "I don't know" about what it was never told. We study two memory channels on a frozen language model, Qwen3-0.6B. The logbook stores each fact with its version outside the weights and is read by a learned reader. The sleep periodically consolidates recent facts into a LoRA adapter with 10 million parameters, using a recipe that replays the facts already consolidated, replays two invented skills together with their abstention boundaries, practices "I don't know" and anchors to the model each sleep starts from. Over eight chained sleeps of 100 facts each, with three seeds, what was learned in the first week holds at an accuracy between 0.88 and 0.97 after seven more sleeps (mean 0.94), and no package falls more than 0.09 below the value it was learned at. An ablation isolates the ingredient. Without fact replay, everything else equal, accuracy on the first week goes from 0.97 to 0.01 in a single sleep. We compare with MEMIT and AlphaEdit, two published knowledge editing methods that write each new fact directly into the model weights, without training it. Editing the same facts week after week, by the eighth week they keep 0.01 and 0.10 of what was learned in the first, and both damage skills the editing never touched. Against a RAG trained with the same budget the logbook ties in accuracy, and the difference is cost, 16 tokens per query against 505, or against 4,514 with a history of 200 old entries. The limit we measure is spurious composition. Given a known entity and a relation never taught for that entity, by the eighth week the model answers a value in 51 to 61% of cases instead of abstaining, and "I don't know" for invented entities drops from 0.97 to 0.64. Evening out how many facts each entity receives, the most obvious intervention, changed nothing (40 answers out of 200 in both conditions). Finally, in a single sleep over real-world facts taken from CounterFact and zsRE, two public benchmarks used as the standard reference to evaluate knowledge editing, the recipe learns the 100 new facts of each completely without worsening perplexity on unseen text.
